WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 124: Martin Van Buren and the Birth of Modern Politics: The Red Fox of Kinderhook

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.040
[Rebecca] Today we turn to America's eighth president, Martin Van Buren.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:22.400
[Rebecca] In American folklore, Van Buren is often remembered for the financial panic of 1837 or for giving the English language the phrase, okay, from his nickname Old Kinderhook.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.960
[Rebecca] But beneath his polished manners and side whiskers was the greatest political architect in American history.

6
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:39.120
[Rebecca] Van Buren took a chaotic, aristocratic system and created the modern political party, with national conventions, campaign rallies, and party discipline.

7
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.360
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Red Fox of Kinderhook.

8
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:57.680
[George] In December 1782, in the small, sleepy Hudson Valley village of Kinderhoek, New York, a boy named Martin Van Buren was born into a world that spoke almost entirely in Dutch.

9
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:07.360
[George] His father, Abraham Van Buren, was an impoverished farmer and tavern keeper who had served as a captain in the New York militia during the Revolutionary War.

10
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:17.440
[George] The family tavern sat along the busy post road between New York City and Albany, serving as the social, commercial, and political heart of the rural community.

11
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:31.200
[George] Growing up behind the tavern counter, young Martin spent his childhood listening to local farmers, itinerant lawyers, and visiting politicians argue over politics, land titles, and elections over mugs of hard cider.

12
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.720
[George] It was an incomparable education in human psychology.

13
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:44.480
[George] Van Buren observed how men were swayed not by abstract philosophical lectures, but by personal loyalty, shared grievances, and skilled persuasion.

14
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:53.360
[George] He learned to read faces, manage difficult personalities, and conceal his own emotions behind a mask of unfailing, polite good cheer.

15
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.640
[George] Van Buren was unique among the early presidents.

16
00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:08.200
[George] He was the first chief executive born after the signing of the Declaration of Independence, making him the very first president who was born a citizen of the United States rather than a British colonial subject.

17
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:17.560
[George] And he remains to this day the only president who learned English as a second language, speaking with a subtle, distinctive Dutch cadence throughout his entire life.

18
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:30.840
[George] Lacking the wealth to attend college, Van Buren entered a local law office at age 14, sweeping floors, copying legal briefs by hand, and preparing to storm the closed citadel of New York politics.

19
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:41.320
[George] In the early 19th century, New York politics was dominated by a handful of wealthy, aristocratic clans, the Livingstons, the Shilers, and the Clintons.

20
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:52.360
[George] These aristocratic elites treated politics as their private hereditary estate, distributing judicial posts and bank charters based on family bloodlines and personal patronage.

21
00:02:52.360 --> 00:03:01.480
[George] Van Buren, representing the disenfranchised small farmers and tradesmen of upstate New York, set out to smash this feudal dynasty.

22
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:10.600
[George] Elected to the New York State Senate in 1812, Van Buren pioneered a revolutionary organizational weapon, the Albany Regency.

23
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.360
[George] It was the first true political machine in American history.

24
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:26.120
[George] Rather than relying on charismatic aristocrats, the Regency was operated by a small, tightly knit council of talented, middle-class professional politicians who met regularly in Albany.

25
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.640
[George] They established the principle of strict party discipline.

26
00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:42.520
[George] Party members in the state legislature met in a private caucus to debate policy, but once a majority vote was taken, every single member was required to vote in lockstep on the legislative floor on pain of expulsion.

27
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:55.440
[George] To communicate with voters, the Regency established an official party newspaper, the Albany Argus, and coordinated dozens of local county newspapers that published identical editorial arguments every week.

28
00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:08.480
[George] When the regency won an election, they systematically rewarded loyal party workers with state jobs, postmasters, canal collectors and judgeships, a practice that became famous as the spoils system.

29
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:20.080
[George] Van Buren openly defended party loyalty, arguing that rewarding dedicated party workers was vastly more democratic than leaving permanent aristocratic elites in office for life.

30
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:26.240
[George] Van Buren's greatest intellectual breakthrough was his radical defense of the political party itself.

31
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:34.640
[George] From the founding of the Republic, political thinkers from George Washington to James Monroe had viewed parties as an evil, factional disease.

32
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:43.200
[George] In his farewell address, Washington had warned that party spirit agitates the community with ill-founded jealousies and false alarms.

33
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:51.360
[George] James Monroe's era of good feelings had sought to eliminate political parties entirely, creating a harmonious one-party state.

34
00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.320
[George] Van Buren looked at the era of good feelings and saw a dangerous disaster.

35
00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:09.520
[George] He argued that when there were no defined political parties based on clear principles, politics inevitably degenerated into selfish, chaotic personal rivalries, corrupt backroom deals, and regional conflicts.

36
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:17.360
[George] Van Buren asserted that organized political parties were not a danger to democracy, but its greatest structural safeguard.

37
00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:28.640
[George] In Van Buren's philosophy, a permanent, healthy opposition party served as a vital watchdog, checking the abuse of power and holding elected officials accountable to the public.

38
00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:35.240
[George] Furthermore, national political parties were the only force capable of overcoming regional factionalism.

39
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:48.120
[George] By uniting citizens across North, South, and West around common economic principles, a national party could bind a sprawling continent together far better than the weak institutions of the federal government.

40
00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:56.280
[George] Van Buren took what the founders viewed as a flaw in human nature and turned it into the central operating engine of American democracy.

41
00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:04.280
[George] In 1824, Van Buren's theories were vindicated by the most controversial presidential election in American history.

42
00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:16.120
[George] In the absence of structured political parties, four candidates from the same party ran against one another Andrew Jackson, John Quincy Adams, William Crawford, and Henry Clay.

43
00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:25.640
[George] Jackson won the most popular votes and electoral votes, but failed to secure an outright majority, throwing the election into the House of Representatives.

44
00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:32.920
[George] There, fourth place finisher Henry Clay threw his support to John Quincy Adams, securing Adams the presidency.

45
00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:40.040
[George] A few days later, Adams appointed Clay Secretary of State, the traditional stepping stone to the White House.

46
00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:46.040
[George] Jackson's furious supporters cried foul, denouncing the transaction as the corrupt bargain.

47
00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:51.400
[George] Watching from the Senate floor, Martin Van Buren recognized a historic opportunity.

48
00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:59.640
[George] The chaotic election proved that without a disciplined party structure, the popular will could be brazenly overridden by elites in Washington.

49
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:05.240
[George] Van Buren traveled to Nashville, Tennessee, to visit Andrew Jackson at the Hermitage.

50
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:16.480
[George] He proposed an audacious, revolutionary political coalition, reviving the old alliance of Thomas Jefferson between the plain Republicans of the North and the planters of the South.

51
00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:24.000
[George] Van Buren offered Jackson the formidable organizational machinery of the Albany Regency and northern working class voters.

52
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:30.640
[George] While Jackson provided his immense, charismatic popularity as the military hero of New Orleans.

53
00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:44.400
[George] Together, they would forge an entirely new national political movement dedicated to states' rights, strict constitutional limits, and the defense of the common man against corporate privilege, the modern Democratic Party.

54
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:54.240
[George] The presidential campaign of 1828 was the first modern political campaign in human history, orchestrated from behind the scenes by Martin Van Buren.

55
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:03.120
[George] Lacking radio, television, or the Internet, Van Buren transformed politics into popular, participatory mass entertainment.

56
00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:14.720
[George] Van Buren recognized that state constitutions across the country had recently eliminated property ownership requirements for voting, extending the franchise to virtually all adult white men.

57
00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:22.240
[George] To mobilize millions of first-time voters, Van Buren created an unprecedented grassroots campaign infrastructure.

58
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.400
[George] Across every state, local hickory clubs were established.

59
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:40.320
[George] Democratic activists organized torchlight parades, brass bands, festive barbecues with free whiskey, and dramatic ceremonies where giant hickory poles, symbolizing old hickory, were erected in town squares.

60
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:51.760
[George] Van Buren established a nationwide network of coordinated partisan newspapers, ensuring that identical campaign slogans, articles, and attacks were printed from Maine to Missouri.

61
00:08:51.760 --> 00:09:07.160
[George] When Adams's supporters attacked Jackson as an uneducated backwoods duelist and murderer, Van Buren turned Jackson's rugged frontier roots into a badge of democratic honor, portraying Adams as an out-of-touch aristocrat who played billiards in the White House.

62
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.960
[George] The result was a democratic revolution.

63
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:20.840
[George] Voter turnout exploded by more than 300%, surging from 360,000 voters in 1824 to over 1.1 million in 1828.

64
00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:32.440
[George] Jackson crushed Adams in a landslide, and Martin Van Buren, winning election as governor of New York in the same cycle, stepped into history as the undisputed kingmaker of the new republic.

65
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:39.720
[George] In March 1829, President Andrew Jackson appointed Martin Van Buren as his Secretary of State.

66
00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:55.880
[George] In Washington, Van Buren quickly earned his famous nicknames, The Little Magician and the Red Fox of Kinderhook, reflecting his uncanny ability to outmaneuver political rivals without ever raising his voice, losing his temper, or making a personal enemy.

67
00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:01.800
[George] His greatest test came during the infamous petticoat affair that paralyzed Jackson's cabinet.

68
00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.040
[George] Washington Society Ladies, led by Vice President John C.

69
00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:16.840
[George] Calhoun's wife, Floride, viciously ostracized Margaret Eaton, the wife of Secretary of War John Eaton, alleging that she was a woman of loose morals.

70
00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:28.360
[George] Jackson, whose own beloved wife Rachel had died of heart failure shortly after the 1828 election following vicious campaign slanders, defended Peggy Eaton with volcanic rage.

71
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:33.640
[George] The cabinet fractured, with Calhoun's supporters refusing to socialize with Eton.

72
00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:41.480
[George] Van Buren, a widower with no wife to dictate his social calendar, saw both a moral duty and a tactical masterstroke.

73
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:47.680
[George] He paid open, courtly attention to Peggy Eaton, hosting lavish dinner parties in her honor.

74
00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:53.840
[George] Then, in 1831, Van Buren engineered a brilliant solution to break the stalemate.

75
00:10:53.840 --> 00:11:03.680
[George] He voluntarily resigned as Secretary of State, allowing Jackson to demand the resignations of Calhoun's hostile allies and reconstitute his entire cabinet.

76
00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:16.400
[George] Jackson was so touched by Van Buren's loyalty and selflessness that he chose the Little Magician as his running mate for vice president in 1832, permanently replacing Calhoun as his political heir.

77
00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:25.760
[George] In 1835, Martin Van Buren formalized another revolutionary political institution, the National Party nominating convention.

78
00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:38.400
[George] Meeting in Baltimore, delegates from across the nation gathered to unanimously nominate Van Buren for president on the Democratic ticket, replacing backroom congressional caucuses with broad party representation.

79
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:47.840
[George] In 1836, Van Buren won the presidency, fulfilling his improbable journey from a Dutch village tavern to the highest office in the land.

80
00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:55.920
[George] During his presidency, Van Buren's supporters popularized the political acronym OK, short for Old Kinderhook.

81
00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:05.120
[George] The phrase was adopted by Democratic clubs across the nation and spread globally, becoming the most universally recognized American word on planet Earth.

82
00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:15.120
[George] While his presidency would be battered by the devastating panic of 1837, Van Buren's true enduring monument was the machinery of American politics.

83
00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:22.320
[George] He transformed the United States from a loose collection of squabbling regional oligarchies into a modern party democracy.

84
00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:34.120
[George] He proved that disciplined political organization, party platforms, and mass voter participation were the true pillars that allow ordinary citizens to triumph over wealth and entrenched privilege.

85
00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:41.960
[George] Martin Van Buren showed the world that in a great republic, democracy is not merely a noble sentiment written on parchment.

86
00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:50.040
[George] It is an art, a science, and a living institution that must be built, organized, and sustained by the people themselves.

87
00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:56.120
[Rebecca] Martin Van Buren built the modern party system that still defines American democracy today.

88
00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:04.840
[Rebecca] By giving ordinary voters a disciplined voice against aristocratic elites, the son of a Kinderhook tavern keeper reshaped how presidents are chosen.

89
00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:09.880
[Rebecca] Next week, we continue with Martin Van Buren's Supreme Domestic Challenge.

90
00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:18.520
[Rebecca] The Panic of 1837, the nation's first great economic depression, and his battle to create an independent treasury.

91
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:22.680
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

